From 5fded82fc4d4e1cf4e65a1064c88fff5ea8371eb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alan Morford <alanmorford@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 07:37:16 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] keys: read each input node after what the previous ones
 delivered

read_input_event() polls every keypad node and reads each one that is
readable into the same buffer - always at pEvents[0] - while adding its
event count to the total. When two nodes have input pending at once, the
second read overwrites the first node's events and the tail of the buffer
is stale.

The power key is the first node on athena (qpnp_pon). On a resume the
press routinely arrives together with events from another node, so the
press was overwritten and luna-displaymanager never saw it: the power
button "often does nothing" and a volume press sometimes "wakes" the
phone instead (the latched events come through on the next read).

Read into pEvents + numEvents with the remaining capacity, and stop when
the buffer is full (the rest is read on the next round).

Reproduced on athena by injecting a power press/release on event0 and
four MSC_SCAN/SYN events on event2 in the same instant, display off:
before 1 of 3 presses lost, after 6 of 6 turned the display on.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/keys/keys_common.c | 12 +++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/keys/keys_common.c b/src/keys/keys_common.c
index 03635b3..470a534 100644
--- a/src/keys/keys_common.c
+++ b/src/keys/keys_common.c
@@ -543,9 +543,19 @@ int read_input_event(InputEvent_t* pEvents, int maxEvents)
         if (fds[n].revents & POLLIN) 
         {
             /* keep looping if get EINTR */
+            if (numEvents >= maxEvents)
+                break;	/* buffer full: the rest waits for the next round */
+
             for (;;) 
             {
-                rd = read(fds[n].fd, pEvents, sizeof(InputEvent_t) * maxEvents);
+                /* Read after what earlier descriptors already delivered. Reading
+                 * every descriptor into pEvents[0] let a second device with
+                 * pending input overwrite the first one's events while the count
+                 * still included both - on resume the power key (qpnp_pon, the
+                 * first node) was routinely overwritten by other keys/switches
+                 * and the press was lost: "the power button often does nothing". */
+                rd = read(fds[n].fd, pEvents + numEvents,
+                          sizeof(InputEvent_t) * (maxEvents - numEvents));
 
                 if (rd > 0) 
                 {
